<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/foundational/training-load-adaptation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the strangest thing about exercise: the training session itself makes you
    <em>worse</em>, not better. Straight after a hard run you are slower, weaker and stiffer
    than before you started. What makes you better is what happens afterwards — during the
    days of eating, sleeping and easy movement, the body does not merely repair the damage; it
    over-rebuilds, preparing for the next assault by raising the ceiling. That over-rebuild is
    <strong>supercompensation</strong>, and it is the single idea behind every training plan
    ever written. Training doesn't make you fitter. <strong>Training plus recovery</strong>
    makes you fitter — the exercise is only the stimulus, the way a question is only the
    stimulus for an answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The timing turns out to matter as much as the effort. Your body is a system that adapts to
    stress it survives, and it adapts <em>during</em> the rest, not the work. So the whole of
    programming — when to train hard, when to rest, when to test — is the art of timing the
    next session onto the peak of the last one's recovery curve. Get the timing wrong in either
    direction and the same work produces either injury or nothing.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="one session, plotted — the dip is fatigue; the bump above baseline is adaptation; the crossings mark the window" widget="plotter" config={superCfg} />

  <p>
    The marked crossings are where the green curve crosses baseline: about <strong>1–2 days of
    fatigue</strong>, then a window of elevated capacity around days 3–5. Train again too early
    and you stack fatigue onto fatigue (the road to overtraining); too late and the adaptation
    has faded; just right and each wave starts higher than the last. Worked example: a novice
    runner does a hard 5 km on Monday. Tuesday she feels leaden — that is the dip. Thursday,
    the same pace feels lighter — that is the peak. If she races on Tuesday and jogs on Sunday,
    she has used the curve backwards twice in one week.
  </p>

  <h2>Health, fitness — and how they differ</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus starts by separating two words people use interchangeably.
    <strong>Health</strong> is a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being — a
    whole-person condition, not a scoreboard. <strong>Fitness</strong> is narrower: the ability
    to meet the demands of an environment or a task. A sumo wrestler is extraordinarily fit for
    his sport and may have hypertension; a marathon runner can have a racing heart rate and
    still be depressed. The relationship between the two is positive but not identical: more
    fitness generally improves health (regular activity cuts cardiovascular-disease risk
    roughly a third, improves mood, sleep and insulin sensitivity), but you can chase fitness
    in ways that damage health — under-eating, overtraining, doping — and you can support
    health while remaining untrained. The practical target is fitness <em>for your life</em>:
    enough capacity that the demands of your days do not exceed you.
  </p>

  <h2>Diet and energy sources: the fuel side of adaptation</h2>
  <p>
    Adaptation is construction, and construction needs materials. The body runs on three fuel
    systems. For the first ~10 seconds of all-out effort, the <strong>ATP–phosphocreatine
    system</strong> powers sprints and lifts. From roughly 10 seconds to 2 minutes, the
    <strong>anaerobic (lactic) system</strong> burns glucose without oxygen, producing the burn
    of a 400 m race. Beyond that, the <strong>aerobic system</strong> burns carbohydrate and
    fat <em>with</em> oxygen — the system that powers everything from a 5 km run to sitting in
    this lesson. Carbohydrate is the high-output fuel (glycogen stored in muscle and liver);
    fat is the vast low-output reserve; protein is mostly a building material, not a fuel.
  </p>
  <p>
    Timing follows function: endurance athletes replenish glycogen with carbohydrate in the
    hours after long sessions (the "window" is real but generous — hours, not minutes), while
    strength training drives a need for protein spread across the day, roughly 1.4–2.0 g per
    kg of body mass for athletes in hard training. Chronic under-fuelling — the trap called
    RED-S, relative energy deficiency in sport — does the opposite of adaptation: stalled
    progress, lost periods in women, stress fractures and immune suppression. You cannot
    supercompensate on an empty site.
  </p>

  <h2>Components of fitness: what exactly are we adapting?</h2>
  <p>
    "Get fitter" is too vague to program. Training science splits fitness into components,
    each with its own adaptation curve. The <strong>health-related components</strong> are
    cardiovascular endurance (the heart–lung delivery system, measured by VO₂max),
    muscular strength (maximum force), muscular endurance (repeated submaximal effort),
    flexibility (range of motion at joints) and body composition (the fat-to-lean ratio).
    The <strong>skill-related components</strong> — agility, balance, coordination, power,
    speed and reaction time — matter most in sport. The point of the list is the principle of
    <strong>specificity</strong>: each component adapts to its own stimulus, so a plan must
    name which components it is buying. Long slow running buys cardiovascular endurance and
    barely touches strength; heavy squatting buys strength and barely touches VO₂max.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "muscle turns to fat" (or fat to muscle). They are different
    tissues; one cannot become the other. What actually happens is simpler — training lapses,
    muscle shrinks from disuse while fat accumulates from unchanged eating. Two processes,
    wrongly narrated as one transformation.
  </p>

  <h2>Fitness testing: protocols and why we bother</h2>
  <p>
    You cannot manage a curve you never measure, which is why testing is part of training, not
    an interruption to it. Standard <strong>test protocols</strong> exist for each component:
    the multi-stage fitness test (beep test) and Cooper 12-minute run estimate aerobic
    endurance; the vertical jump or broad jump estimate power; the handgrip dynamometer and
    1-repetition-maximum tests measure strength; the sit-and-reach measures flexibility; skinfolds or bioelectrical impedance estimate body composition.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>reasons for testing</strong> go beyond curiosity. Tests (1) establish baselines,
    so training loads can be set as percentages of what you can actually do; (2) track progress,
    turning adaptation from a feeling into a number; (3) reveal weaknesses that limit
    performance — a sprinter with poor flexibility is a hamstring tear waiting to happen;
    (4) motivate, because plotted improvement is the most powerful adherence drug known to
    sport; and (5) help talent identification and goal-setting. The rules that make a test
    meaningful: validity (it measures the component it claims to), reliability (same
    conditions, same result), and sensible protocol — warm up, standardise the conditions,
    and never schedule a max test the day after your hardest session, or you will measure your
    fatigue and call it your fitness.
  </p>

  <h2>Principles of training and overload</h2>
  <p>
    The supercompensation curve generates the classic training principles as corollaries.
    <strong>Progressive overload</strong>: the stimulus must exceed what the body is used to,
    and must keep growing as you adapt — the same 20 press-ups that built you at week one
    maintain you by week ten. Overload can be raised by frequency, intensity, time or type
    (the <strong>FITT</strong> levers). <strong>Specificity</strong>: you get what you train
    for, right down to the muscle fibres and energy systems recruited.
    <strong>Reversibility</strong>: use it or lose it — endurance adaptations begin to fade
    within two weeks of stopping, strength slower but surely. <strong>Variation</strong> (and
    its planned form, periodization): the stimulus must change over months, because a plateau
    is the body announcing it has finished adapting to today's question. <strong>Individuality</strong>:
    the same session produces different curves in different bodies — genetics sets the slope.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "no pain, no gain" — that soreness equals effectiveness.
    Delayed-onset muscle soreness is a rough marker of novelty to the muscle, not of growth,
    and chasing it pushes people to stack fatigue faster than they recover. Pain that is
    sharp, one-sided or in a joint is not training stimulus; it is a damage report.
  </p>

  <h2>Methods of training</h2>
  <p>
    Each method is a tool aimed at a component. <strong>Continuous training</strong> (steady
    running, cycling, swimming for 20+ minutes) builds the aerobic base. <strong>Fartlek</strong>
    — Swedish for "speed play" — mixes paces inside one continuous run, teaching the body to
    change gears. <strong>Interval training</strong> alternates work and relief with
    precision: 6 × 400 m fast with 90 s jog recoveries, for example, accumulates more
    high-quality time at high oxygen uptake than a flat tempo run. High-intensity interval
    training (HIIT) compresses this to short, near-maximal bursts and delivers aerobic gains
    in a fraction of the session time. <strong>Weight (resistance) training</strong> builds
    strength and power through progressive loading — typically 1–5 repetitions at heavy loads
    for strength, 8–12 for muscle growth, 15+ for muscular endurance. <strong>Plyometrics</strong>
    (jumping, bounding) train elastic power. <strong>Circuit training</strong> rotates stations
    to blend endurance and strength efficiently, and <strong>flexibility work</strong> — static
    stretching held 20–30 s, or dynamic mobility drills — maintains the range that lets the
    other methods run safely.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked programme sketch shows the principles meeting the methods: a beginner wants
    general fitness, so she trains four days a week — two continuous 30-minute runs (aerobic
    base), one full-body resistance session (strength), one interval session (VO₂max). Load
    rises about 10% per week, never 10% per day, because tendons and bone remodel slower than
    muscle and cardiovascular fitness — which is exactly why "too much, too soon" injures
    connective tissue first. Every fourth week drops back to consolidate: a planned dip that
    is simply the supercompensation curve drawn across weeks instead of days.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The plot above is a picture of Banister's <strong>fitness–fatigue model</strong>, the
    oldest formal account of training: every session leaves two after-effects, one negative
    (fatigue, decaying fast) and one positive (fitness, decaying slowly), and your performance
    is their difference. It explains at a stroke why tapering before competition works — cut
    the training load and fatigue evaporates within days while the slow-decaying fitness
    remains, so performance rises. The model is a simplification (real adaptations are dozens
    of processes with different time constants), but it remains the working mental model of
    every strength coach and the basis of "training-load" analytics in elite sport.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantitatively, the timescales are well studied. Neural strength gains appear within 2–4
    weeks (better recruitment, before bigger fibres); muscle cross-section needs 6–8+ weeks;
    VO₂max responds over 4–12 weeks of aerobic work; tendon and bone collagen remodels over
    months — hence tendon injuries presenting as the classic error of an enthusiastic first
    month. Detraining is asymmetric: VO₂max can fall ~5–10% within 2–4 weeks of total rest,
    while strength persists longer and returns faster when retrained (muscle nuclei, once
    gained, appear to persist).
  </p>
  <p>
    The expert edge cases are about stacking. <strong>Functional overreaching</strong> is a
    deliberate, short overload block where fatigue accumulates for 1–2 weeks followed by a
    recovery peak — the curve used on purpose. <strong>Non-functional overreaching</strong> is
    the same thing done too long: weeks of stalled performance that weeks of rest can still
    fix. <strong>Overtraining syndrome</strong> is the endpoint: months of depression of mood,
    sleep, immunity and performance that rest alone may not reverse. The monitoring toolkit
    for staying on the right side of the line — morning resting heart rate, heart-rate
    variability, sleep quality, mood questionnaires — exists because the curve punishes greed
    first in the knees, shoulders and motivation.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Banister's fitness–fatigue (impulse–response) model; Bompa, Periodization: Theory and Methodology of Training; ACSM Guidelines for Exercise Testing and Prescription; Mountjoy et al., IOC consensus on RED-S.</p>
</TopicPage>
